memory-model reconstruction -- the map->tile fill that patch 286's renderer 9200 samples.

DIAGNOSIS CORRECTION.  The 256x256 colormap tile at DAT_0000_3918 that the oracle-proven terrain
renderer FUN_0000_9200 samples is NOT filled by ad1e (a general 2D point/span primitive rasterizer,
op 0x40, never posted) as previously hypothesised.  It is built by the two-plane VOXEL rasterizer
FUN_0000_6980 (dispatched by op 0x08 -> handler 0x10e0 = `call 8df0; call 3931`; 3931 = 85d0 camera
+ 6980 build), which walks the map height plane at [0x85bc] and colour plane at [0x85bc+0x100000]
through the camera and writes terrain colour runs (via the per-depth write-pointer table 4a60[],
initialised to DAT_3918 + row*38ed) into the tile.  op 0x08 is NEVER posted by the port's render
dispatch (only op 0x0c fires once) so the tile stays 0 -> black terrain.  6980 + its ray-table/
height-LUT builder 395e were decompiled but never exercised, so their flat32 base-losses were latent.

All asm-verified vs re_out/fist_image.bin:
  * 395e (0x395e): the LUT dest pointer (asm 3979 `mov edi,ds:0x3909`) and the ray-table copies /
    reads at 0x4624/0x3e24/0x3a24/0x4224 (asm `[esi*4+off]`) were Ghidra base-lost (bare small ints
    -> near-NULL writes -> SIGSEGV).  Rebuilt as g_mem+fist_ext_base + off + idx*4; the LUT dest is
    the host-pointer value *(uint*)(base+0x3909).
  * 6980 (0x6980): (a) the per-heading SIN/COS projection table at image 0x9450 (same base-loss class
    as patch 286's 8120: `&FUN_0000_944b`+5 host fn-ptr, bare 0x9454 host addr, and `&DAT_9650`+idx*4
    4x-over-scaled) -> byte arithmetic on the module base; (b) the ray tables 0x4224/0x4624 base;
    (c) the SELF-MODIFIED height->screenrow LUT lookups (asm `mov eax,0x7fffffff`(SMC=[0x3909]|bl);
    `add al,[esi+ecx]`(=+height); `mov al,[eax]`) which Ghidra baked as CONCAT31(0x7fffff,h-1) ->
    rebuilt from the SMC value _uRam00006add computed at entry (asm 6a04); (d) the backward-pass
    y-buffer/write-ptr tables 0x4e5f/0x4a5c (Ghidra used undefined4* *4 scaling vs asm byte/dword
